UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 8, Lesson 1: Loud and soft: dynamics', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Dynamics are how loud or soft music is. The word piano, written p, means soft, and forte, written f, means loud. A crescendo is a gradual getting louder, shown with a widening shape, and a diminuendo is a gradual getting softer. Changing dynamics makes music exciting, like a story where something is far away and then gets closer. Composers write dynamics on the music so players know when to change. Remember that soft does not mean slow and loud does not mean fast.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same arts idea to a new artwork or performance, then compare two choices for clarity of effect.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPlan a short piece about a thunderstorm. Where would you use piano, forte, crescendo and diminuendo? Write the dynamics in order.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Piano for the first quiet drips, a crescendo as the storm comes closer, forte for the loud thunder and a diminuendo as it moves away.'' Each dynamic matches a part of the story.\n\nWATCH FOR\nThinking that dynamics mean fast and slow. Dynamics are about loud and soft only, and changing the speed is a different idea called tempo.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose a short rhyme or song and write a dynamics plan with p, f, a crescendo and a diminuendo above the words. Perform it using your voice or body percussion at a safe volume, and explain how the dynamic changes make it more interesting.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one artistic choice you changed and why. Movement or instrument work is optional, adult-supervised and can be described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Creative Arts","learning_intention":"Dynamics are how loud or soft music is. The word piano, written p, means soft, and forte, written f, means loud. A crescendo is a gradual getting louder, shown with a widening shape, and a diminuendo is a gradual getting softer. Changing dynamics makes music exciting, like a story where something is far away and then gets closer. Composers write dynamics on the music so players know when to change. Remember that soft does not mean slow and loud does not mean fast.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Explore and learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: scrap paper, crayons, safe classroom instruments or clear movement space with an adult nearby"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["CA2-MUS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose a short rhyme or song and write a dynamics plan with p, f, a crescendo and a diminuendo above the words. Perform it using your voice or body percussion at a safe volume, and explain how the dynamic changes make it Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using dynamics on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Thinking that dynamics mean fast and slow.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Piano for the first quiet drips, a crescendo as the storm comes closer, forte for the loud thunder and. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Thinking that dynamics mean fast and slow. Dynamics are about loud and soft only, and changing the speed is a different idea called tempo. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating dynamics on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with dynamics, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about dynamics with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for dynamics. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for dynamics. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson.","''Piano for the first quiet drips, a crescendo as the storm comes closer, forte for the loud thunder and a diminuendo as","Thinking that dynamics mean fast and slow.","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material."],"question":"Plan a short piece about a thunderstorm. Where would you use piano, forte, crescendo and diminuendo? Write the dynamics in order. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for dynamics; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["''Piano for the first quiet drips, a crescendo as the storm comes closer, forte for the loud thunder and a diminuendo as","Careful use of piano (soft) and explain why it matters with clear evidence.","Thinking that dynamics mean fast and slow.","Dynamics are how loud or soft music is."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with dynamics?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named piano (soft) and explain why it matters, not by copying the mo