d a purpose-linked reason for dynamics. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with dynamics, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Thinking that dynamics mean fast and slow.. Correction: Strong answer: ''Piano for the first quiet drips, a crescendo as the storm comes closer, forte for the loud thunder and. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent artwork or performance notes and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-arts-y3-w08-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Creative Arts alignment using catalogue outcome codes (CA2-*); content-point mapping remains pending. First Nations arts content requires local Aboriginal educator or Custodian guidance before acceptance; do not invent sacred designs.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise dynamics.","Read the mission before you start making or performing.","Keep the audience effect of your choice in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not name an effect clearly."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the element or technique that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore elements, safety or respectful practice.","Prefer a named artistic reason over a guess that merely looks colourful."],"submit":["Confirm that your choice and reason are both present.","Proofread element names and safety notes.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact element, technique or cue before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key artistic choices.","Say what each choice does for the artwork or audience.","A final praise word alone is weaker than a named effect."],"teach2":["Compare a weak choice with a stronger one.","Change the element or technique, not only the decoration.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh subject or piece that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original work notes and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill on new material."]},"subject":"Music","success_criteria":["I can use dynamics with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify piano (soft) and explain why it matters.","I can practise forte (loud) independently on a new prompt.","I can notice crescendo needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Dynamics are how loud or soft music is.","options":["Dynamics are how loud or soft music is.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about dynamics with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: a piece starts piano with quiet taps like footsteps far away. The taps get louder with a crescendo as someone comes closer, reach forte when the person is at the door and then quickly drop back to piano for a whispered knock. The tempo stays the same all the way through; only the loudness changes.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPlan a short piece about a thunderstorm. Where would you use piano, forte, crescendo and diminuendo? Write the dynamics in order.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Piano for the first quiet drips, a crescendo as the storm comes closer, forte for the loud thunder and a diminuendo as it moves away.'' Each dynamic matches a part of the story.","explain":"Dynamics are how loud or soft music is. The word piano, written p, means soft, and forte, written f, means loud. A crescendo is a gradual getting louder, shown with a widening shape, and a diminuendo is a gradual getting softer. Changing dynamics makes music exciting, like a story where something is far away and then gets closer. Composers write dynamics on the music so players know when to change. Remember that soft does not mean slow and loud does not mean fast.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Thinking that dynamics mean fast and slow. Dynamics are about loud and soft only, and changing the speed is a different idea called tempo. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Piano and forte.","caption":"Soft and loud.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text 