"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Crescendo</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">gradually louder</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Diminuendo</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">gradually softer</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison.","A guess with no artistic reason.","A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about dynamics with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThinking that dynamics mean fast and slow. Dynamics are about loud and soft only, and changing the speed is a different idea called tempo. Here is a more helpful example: Example: a piece starts piano with quiet taps like footsteps far away. The taps get louder with a crescendo as someone comes closer, reach forte when the person is at the door and then quickly drop back to piano for a whispered knock. The tempo stays the same all the way through; only the loudness changes.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice weakens the work, then write a stronger choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: ''Piano for the first quiet drips, a crescendo as the storm comes closer, forte for the loud thunder and a diminuendo as it moves away.'' Each dynamic matches a part of the story.","explain":"Use an intentionally weak or disrespectful arts choice as a comparison. Name the specific error, then show a clearer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make intentional visual, music, drama or dance choices and name their effect, rather than saying an artwork is simply “nice”. Keep every claim linked to an element or technique.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the elements, techniques or performance choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For movement or instruments, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby and stop if anything feels unsafe. You may always describe a choice instead of performing it. One deliberate artistic choice with a named effect is more useful than a longer praise sentence.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the decoration without changing the artistic idea does not fix the error. Thinking that dynamics mean fast and slow. Dynamics are about loud and soft only, and changing the speed is a different idea called tempo. Name the element or technique and its effect, not just decoration.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example.","options":["A comment that only says it looks nice with no element named.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no artistic reason.","Use the same arts idea in a new artwork, song, drama or dance, not a copy of the example."],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about dynamics with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nDynamics are how loud or soft music is. The word piano, written p, means soft, and forte, written f, means loud. A crescendo is a gradual getting louder, shown with a widening shape, and a diminuendo is a gradual getting softer. Changing dynamics makes music exciting, like a story where something is far away and then gets closer. Composers write dynamics on the music so players know when to change. Remember that soft does not mean slow and loud does not mean fast. Now choose a different subject or piece to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same arts idea as the model, but with differen